A||||11||jerpro||Scared to DEATH!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||jerpro||05-20-2001||06:12 PM||||I took my car to valvoline for an oil change (I felt lazy and I had just got a payckeck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) last night. They did a fairly quick job (too qick to be true) but I thought nothing of it and went home (about 2 imn away) and parked the car untill this morning. <BR>I was late for church so I quikly dressed up hopped (better said dropped) into mmy fiero and took off to chuch.<BR>While I am driving I suddenly smell some burning stench and I start looking for burnt cables in my dash or any warning light in my dashboard. NOTHING. I then look through my rearview mmirror and I see bunches of smoke coming out of my engine. and I also see some kids in a celica laughing their heads off. As any other fiero enthusiast I did not think it was that funny so I jerked the wheel and pulled over. I opened the engine compartment and saw that the stupid bastards forgot to put the engine oil cap back on!!!!!! @!#@!%@% F@$k the all!!!!<BR>I had to leave my car there in the mimddle of the highway aaaaaaallll alone and go call my brother so he would bring me another oil cap from my other fiero. I am mmad and just had to bent off. Sorry I let it out on you guys.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||DrRodey||05-20-2001||06:26 PM||||I had the same thing happen to me at a tires plus.. ended up bitching to the manager and got three free oil changes and 25% off my next set of tires.||||reg||1
Z||000002||KissMySSFiero||05-20-2001||09:21 PM||||whats the good at bitchin to get a free oil change.  would you want to take your car back.  i would complain and try to get your money back.  and if possible get the guys address that worked on your car.  then go to his house, take him in the backyard and break his legs.   =Þ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||05-20-2001||09:41 PM||||It's not just the quickie lube places that do that. My neighbors carried their GTP to the dealership for service before going on vacation. After stopping for fuel a few hours later, he discovered the car was a quart low on oil and the filler cap was missing. All they got was a new cap and an apology.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WampusCat||05-20-2001||10:11 PM||||My friend took her new pickup to Kmart for its first oil change.  On the way home the oil drain plug fell out and all the oil ran out onto the freeway.  Luckily she pulled over and switched it off as soon as the oil light came on.  Kmart paid for the tow and another oil change.  I'm sure there are thousands of oil changes done perfectly at these places every day, but I'll still do my own.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroFanaticus2||05-20-2001||10:23 PM||||I had this happen to me too at a local dealership.  Fortunatly I went to the grociery store right afterwards and usually put the grocieries into the spare area in the engine compartment.  When I opened the lid and saw my oil cap sitting in the carpeted area I was rather surprised.  I didn't think much of it then and just put it back on.  WELL, I went back to the smae place a week or so later to have them take a look at my brakes.  One of the older guys there took me back to look at a caliper that was broken, he showed me where it was broke and explained it all to me, so I set up an appointment to have it fixed.  The day came for it to be replaced and the same guy who did my oil job was going to replace my caliper.  He said it would probably take about an hour or so, so I walked to the computer store to get some extra memory.  I came back to the auto shop only about 20 minutes after I had left and my car was sitting outside.  I went in and asked if it had been worked on and he said the caliper wasn't broken, it was just 'stuck'.  Luckily he only wanted 20 something dollars for his time.  I haven't gone back there and have told everyone I can not to go there.  I went and have my calipers looked at somewhere else, had it replaced and the old part given back to me - in it's broken condition.<BR>IF YOU ARE EVER IN FORT ATKSINSON, WISCONSIN ---NEVER NEVER NEVER GO TO KRENTZ' AUTOMART!!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||drednot||05-21-2001||07:38 PM||||My friend had the same thing happen to his toyota celica.  only it did not start smoking, it did however blow all of the oil out and that was the end of that.||||reg||11
Z||000007||thomas_l||05-21-2001||08:46 PM||||I had the Texaco quick lube I always go to raised my 97 Grand Marquis up to do a rotate & balance - without disabling the air suspension. Then they had the stones to pull it out and park it (ready!) with the rear collapsed. I really wasn't that mad about the screw-up. I have done a few screw-ups myself. What sent me into orbit was the manager telling me "oh they'll fill right up" (I have had to install new bags on cars others did this to). After that remark I told him "OK that's nice but you now own those airbags for the next few months. I hope you're as lucky as you are wrong."||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||05-23-2001||10:18 PM||||I have had some good luck with auto service places.  One in particular was an OTASCO shop in Wichita, KS.  I think OTASCO is out of business now.<P>I also had reasonably good luck with the Pontiac dealerships in Wichita; recall work, clutch replacement  and A/C work in particular.  During one recall stop, they locked the keys in my car; at least they admitted what happened, so I dropped off the extra keys for them.  For the clutch replacement, they left the O2 sensor unplugged - it happens.  For the A/C work, I told them up front to save the old parts - they forgot to save the old compressor, and had to run and get it out of the scrap barrel.  Lack of communication seems to be the biggest headache.<P>I don't have a problem with things going wrong, as long as they <B>try</B> to make things right with me.  My latest experience was with a dealership in Everett, WA - I foolishly took a clutch replacement job to Clyde-Revolting (or something like that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).  What a fiasco: incompetence+ dishonesty+ lack of communications+ lack of respect for the customer = extremely unhappy customer + no repeat business from same.<P>GM will get the full story, as soon as I write it up.  Enough said.    ||||reg||4
Z||000009||LarryB||05-23-2001||10:42 PM||||One of my techs had the same thing happen at a Jiffy Lube with her year-old Camaro w/5.7L engine; the guy who changed the oil didn't put the plug back in properly. Unfortunately for her, on the way home the oil all dumped out and the engine seized. <P>If you have the space available to do it you should change your own oil anyways, not only will the job get done right but it gives you a great chance to take a good look underneath and check for potential or developing problems. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by WampusCat:<BR><B>My friend took her new pickup to Kmart for its first oil change.  On the way home the oil drain plug fell out and all the oil ran out onto the freeway.  Luckily she pulled over and switched it off as soon as the oil light came on.  Kmart paid for the tow and another oil change.  I'm sure there are thousands of oil changes done perfectly at these places every day, but I'll still do my own.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||05-23-2001||11:21 PM||||The body shop couldn't figure out how to get into my trunk(electric)without a key,so I called a locksmith to drill out the lock,and he drilled right through the fiberglass structure;what a moron.<BR>Can't wait to just get the car back into my driveway.the last time I paid someplace to do work was four(4) years ago and it sucked then also.<BR>Now I think that if I don't feel like spending the time or energy to work on my Fiero that it will just have sit parked until I do;It's not good economy to have to pay for something,then have to do it youself.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||09:36 AM||||Back when I had my dodge van several of us at work were taking our cars to a local repair shop for basic work and state inspections, we were all happy with what they were doing.<P>  I took the van in for inspection and thought, maybe if I throw some work their way they wont get petty on the inspection and nickle and dime me for stuff. It was due for an oil change so I had them do that at the same time.<P>  about a month later I noticed oil spots on my driveway, and the faint smell of burning oil while driving. I found oil leaking by the oil filter, tried to twist it on a little tighter but it wouldnt budge, so I figured maybe I got an oil filter with a bad seal, so i planned on letting it go till next time i changed the oil.<P>  when I finally replaced it I discovered it wasnt the seal, the mechanic had put the oil filter ON with the pnumatic oil filter wrench, and it had a pin hole in it from the giant socket like thing. In fact the whole end of the oil filter was slightly twisted - he must have torqued it on to about 80 ft lbs.<P>    That was the last time I let anyone change my oil, or do any work I can do myself. It was also the last time I went to that garage. When a store or business gets me PO'd I dont go back and complain.<P>   I simply never go back there again. Life if too short for me to be their quality ensurance inspector.||||reg||1
